From: OpenMANET Pi 5 port <noreply@openmanet.org>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: usb: DIAGNOSTIC - rx_burst_size override and RX urb error logging

NOT AN UPSTREAM PATCH. Diagnostic instrumentation for the RTL8822BU
(0bda:b812) SuperSpeed failure on Raspberry Pi 5 / RP1 xHCI, where an active
scan dies with "failed to do USB write, ret=-19" (-ENODEV) followed by an
xHCI port reset, while the identical adapter scans fine at High Speed on the
same host.

After excluding the probe-time USB2->USB3 mode switch and rtw_usb_dynamic_rx_
agg_v2() (which is 8821A/8812A only - 8822B uses _v1, which is NOT speed
dependent), exactly ONE speed-dependent register setting remains for this chip:

  rtw_usb_init_burst_pkt_len()  usb.c
    USB_SPEED_SUPER -> BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_1024
    USB_SPEED_HIGH  -> BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_512
  written to REG_RXDMA_MODE (0x0290) bits 5:4.

Comparing High Speed against SuperSpeed varies both the link speed and this
burst size at once. The rx_burst_size parameter lets the burst size be varied
ALONE, at SuperSpeed, which is the controlled experiment:

  rtw88_usb.rx_burst_size=512   at SuperSpeed

If that is stable, the 1024-byte RX DMA burst is implicated and the link speed
is not. If it still fails, the burst size is exonerated.

The second hunk exists because the RX urb error path is silent: every status
in the case list (including -EOVERFLOW, i.e. babble, and -EPROTO) falls
through to a bare break with no log line, and rtw_usb_rx_resubmit() is called
only on the success path and from rtw_usb_setup_rx(). So RTW_USB_RXCB_NUM (4)
transient RX errors permanently retire RX with nothing whatsoever in dmesg -
which is very likely why the first fault is invisible today and only the
downstream -ENODEV writes are seen. This hunk only logs; it deliberately does
NOT change the resubmit behaviour, since that would be a behavioural change
rather than a diagnostic.

Revert both hunks once the cause is identified.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
@@ -19,6 +19,11 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(switch_usb_mode,
 		 "Set to N to disable switching to USB 3 mode to avoid potential interference in the 2.4 GHz band (default: Y)");
 
+static int rtw_rx_burst_size = -1;
+module_param_named(rx_burst_size, rtw_rx_burst_size, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(rx_burst_size,
+		 "DIAGNOSTIC: force the RX DMA burst size (1024, 512 or 64). -1 (default) picks it from the USB link speed, as before.");
+
 #define RTW_USB_MAX_RXQ_LEN	512
 
 struct rtw_usb_txcb {
@@ -724,6 +729,14 @@
 			rtw_err(rtwdev, "status %d\n", urb->status);
 			break;
 		}
+		/* DIAGNOSTIC: the cases above are otherwise swallowed with no log
+		 * line, and the URB is never resubmitted - rtw_usb_rx_resubmit() is
+		 * called only on the success path and from rtw_usb_setup_rx(). So
+		 * RTW_USB_RXCB_NUM (4) transient RX errors silently retire RX for
+		 * good. Make them visible. Behaviour is otherwise unchanged.
+		 */
+		rtw_err(rtwdev, "rx urb error, status=%d actual_length=%u\n",
+			urb->status, urb->actual_length);
 		if (skb)
 			dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
 	}
@@ -804,7 +817,13 @@
 
 	rxdma = BIT_DMA_BURST_CNT | BIT_DMA_MODE;
 
-	if (speed == USB_SPEED_SUPER)
+	if (rtw_rx_burst_size == 1024)
+		burst_size = BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_1024;
+	else if (rtw_rx_burst_size == 512)
+		burst_size = BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_512;
+	else if (rtw_rx_burst_size == 64)
+		burst_size = BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_64;
+	else if (speed == USB_SPEED_SUPER)
 		burst_size = BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_1024;
 	else if (speed == USB_SPEED_HIGH)
 		burst_size = BIT_DMA_BURST_SIZE_512;
